3482818603515625, -0.0113983154296875, -0.042724609375, -0.0174560546875, 0.01898193359375, -0.00872802734375, -0.0021953582763671875, 0.024200439453125, -0.0026950836181640625, 0.0188446044921875, -0.0103759765625, -0.01450347900390625, 2.7120113372802734e-05, 0.004802703857421875, -0.0033416748046875, -0.01012420654296875, -0.00579071044921875, 0.0204315185546875, 0.0276031494140625, -0.0260467529296875, 0.014068603515625, -0.0032520294189453125, -0.02435302734375, 0.0166168212890625, 0.0159454345703125, -0.005611419677734375, 0.0238494873046875, -0.0081024169921875, 0.017242431640625, -0.018890380859375, 0.0005908012390136719, 0.00043511390686035156, -0.002498626708984375, 0.00174713134765625, 0.01558685302734375, 0.04644775390625, -0.0163116455078125, 0.01044464111328125, -0.004810333251953125, -0.00826263427734375, 0.0172271728515625, -0.00617218017578125, 0.0056610107421875, -0.052398681640625, -0.007465362548828125, -0.003570556640625, 0.018310546875, 0.0006775856018066406, 0.01076507568359375, -0.00598907470703125, 0.025604248046875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Classical mean-field theory for large random spiking networks often asserts that macroscopic network behavior is determined solely by aggregate statistics\u2014specifically, the *weak convergence* of the associated input measures for each synaptic type\u2014as the fast-synapse limit is approached. In this framework, only the global rates or distributions of excitation and inhibition are considered relevant, and the fine timing of individual spikes is presumed to \u201caverage out.\u201d In practice, this means only overall quantities like spike counts or mean firing rates are predicted by mean-field statistics, supposedly independent of precise spike timing.\n\n**Summary of Main Findings (When and Why the Counterexample Applies):**\n- *Order-sensitivity emerges in threshold-reset neuronal networks with sparse random connectivity and rapidly decaying (\u201cdelta-like\u201d) synaptic kernels.*\n- *Even if population-averaged excitation/inhibition rates match (\u201cweak convergence\u201d), swapping the order of excitation/inhibition pulses can systematically alter which neurons fire\u2014and thus the total spike count and mean firing rate.*\n- *This order effect does **not** arise in densely connected networks or with slow, temporally extended synapses. It is specific to point-process (spike-based) systems with hard thresholds and fast input integration.*  \n**In summary:** The claim that only aggregate input statistics matter at the macroscopic level can fail, and the specific order of spike arrivals can have a nonvanishing effect, whenever synaptic integration is fast and connectivity is sparse.\n\nTo clarify these concepts, we first introduce the key definitions, notate the main quantities, and provide anchoring examples. After each technical specification, we briefly recap its role or import to help reader orientation.\n\n---\n\n**1. Notation, Terminology, and Model Specification**\n\n**Plain-language preview:**  \n*Weak convergence* signals \u201cbulk similarity\u201d in input spike trains\u2014the overall time-averaged impact of incoming synaptic activity matches across compared networks, even though exact spike times may differ. *Componentwise weak convergence* strengthens this, demanding the bulk matching occurs separately within each class of inputs (e.g., excitation independent from inhibition).\n\n- **Example:** Consider a neuron receiving two streams of spikes: one excitatory, one inhibitory. If each delivers 1000 spikes in 1 second, but the excitatory spikes arrive early and inhibitory spikes late in one experiment, while in another they are interleaved, the *componentwise* input measures (counting all excitation together, all inhibition together) can still be identical in the sense of aggregate rates, even though the timing sequences differ. *Weak convergence* would collapse all inputs into a single measure, but *componentwise* treats them separately for each type.\n\n**Relation and distinction:**  \nThus, *weak convergence* addresses total input, regardless of origin, while *componentwise weak convergence* applies weak convergence **independently** to each biologically distinct input class. This ensures not only that the total synaptic drive matches in the average, but that the composition by type does too.\n\n**Link to biological observables:**  \nIn neuroscientific terms, \u201caggregate input measures\u201d reflect biologically tractable quantities such as total synaptic current or the time-averaged excitatory/inhibitory firing rates impinging on a neuron. Mean-field theory posits that these bulk quantities alone suffice to determine the network\u2019s large-scale behavior, not the precise sequence of individual spike arrivals.\n\n**Main Notation and Sign Conventions (appearing throughout):**\n- **$N$:** Number of neurons in the network.\n- **$q_N$:** Out-degree, i.e., number of outgoing synapses per neuron. We assume $q_N\\to\\infty$ and $q_N/N\\to 0$ as $N\\to\\infty$ (sparse but highly sampled regime).\n- **$\\lambda_N$:** Mean out-degree in alternative random graph models; often, $q_N = \\lambda_N$ unless specified.\n- **$a > 0$:** Amplitude (strength) of an excitatory synaptic pulse. Always **positive** by convention.\n- **$b < 0$:** Amplitude (strength) of an inhibitory pulse. Always **negative** by convention.\n- **$K(t)$:** Synaptic kernel; the temporal shape of the postsynaptic current per incoming spike. In the *fast-synapse* limit, $K(t)$ shrinks to have small support (delta-like) with area preserved.\n- **$x$:** Membrane potential just prior to receiving any input sequence.\n- **$\\theta$:** The voltage threshold for spike emission.\n- **$\\tau$:** Temporal delay between input pulses.\n- **$\\pi_N, \\pi$:** Proportions of neuron types (e.g., excitatory/inhibitory) in finite and infinite networks.\n- **$O_P(\\cdot)$, $o_{L^1}(1)$:** Probability and mean asymptotic notations.\n\n**Recap:**  \nThis notation enables us to precisely distinguish between input pulse types, their strengths, synapse kinetics, and population statistics\u2014clarifying claims about equivalence or inequivalence at the level of firing outcomes.\n\nThe network is modeled as a block-structured random directed graph satisfying Dale's principle: Each neuron is strictly excitatory or inhibitory, and connections honor this distinction. Neuron dynamics follow a threshold-reset rule: On reaching $\\theta$, a neuron spikes and immediately resets its membrane potential.\n\n---\n\n**2. Definitions: Weak and Componentwise Weak Convergence**\n\n**Summary recap:**  \nThe mathematical backbone of mean-field reasoning is the assumption that only the limiting (weak) or componentwise weak convergence of the input trains matters for outputs\u2014so long as global statistics per synapse class match, so should outcomes.\n\n- **Weak convergence (formal):**  \n  Let $\\mu_N$ denote the empirical measure representing the arrival times of spikes (e.g., a sum of Dirac deltas at the spike times). We say $\\mu_N \\to \\mu$ weakly if for every bounded continuous function $f$, $\\int f(t)\\, d\\mu_N(t) \\to \\int f(t)\\, d\\mu(t)$. Practically, overall effects on membrane potential or synaptic current become indistinguishable as $N \\rightarrow \\infty$ if only weak convergence is imposed.\n- **Componentwise weak convergence:**  \n  Suppose synaptic inputs segregate into $J$ types (e.g., excitation and inhibition). For each type $j$, write $\\mu_N^{(j)}$. Componentwise weak convergence means $\\mu_N^{(j)} \\to \\mu^{(j)}$ weakly for all $j$. In effect, both excitatory and inhibitory drives match in population-average sense, even if their spike times differ.\n\n**Example (beyond single neuron):**  \nSuppose two networks of $N$ neurons both deliver mean excitatory rate $\\rho_E$ and mean inhibitory rate $\\rho_I$ per neuron, with each neuron receiving many input pulses per unit time but with the detailed timing sequences re-ordered between networks\u2014componentwise weak convergence holds if the totals per input type match in the limit.\n\n**Recap:**  \nMean-field theory, thus, claims that \u201cas long as each neuron\u2019s excitatory and inhibitory input rates converge to their respective means, fine details, such as spike timing or order, do not affect macroscopic predictions.\u201d\n\n---\n\n**3. Main Result: Failure of Mean-Field Equivalence Under Fast, Sparse Synapses**\n\n**Transition and recap:**  \nWith the conceptual and technical background rehearsed, we can now state the primary result. The standard mean-field equivalence\u2014where \u201cweak convergence implies similar firing\u201d\u2014breaks in certain fast, sparse networks.\n\n**Core finding:**  \nIn threshold-reset spiking networks with both *sparse connectivity* and *rapidly decaying (delta-like) synaptic kernels*, even *componentwise* weak convergence is insufficient to guarantee equivalence of \u201cpopulation-level firing statistics,\u201d such as the mean firing rate. Crucially, the *temporal sequence* (order) in which an excitatory ($a$) and an inhibitory ($b$) spike impinge\u2014within an interval so small as to be indistinguishable to mean-field\u2014can tilt some neurons just above or just below threshold, leading to persistent, nonvanishing differences in which neurons emit a spike.\n\n**Recap of implication:**  \nClassic mean-field arguments ignore spike order and so can systematically mispredict macroscopic quantities under these network and synaptic conditions.\n\n---\n\n**4. Illustrative Example: Excitatory-First vs. Inhibitory-First Input**\n\n**Transition:**  \nTo anchor this \u201corder effect,\u201d we analyze the response of a single threshold-reset neuron to two closely-spaced pulses (one excitatory, one inhibitory), then connect the outcome to population-level effects.\n\nSuppose a neuron has initial membrane potential $x$ and threshold $\\theta$. Consider two input protocols, identical in aggregate (componentwise) input measures as $\\tau\\downarrow 0$ (i.e., as pulses become indistinguishable in mean-field), but differing in the order of arrivals:\n\n| Protocol              | $t = -\\tau$       | $t = 0$            | Post-Input Voltage        | Spikes?                                 |\n|-----------------------|-------------------|--------------------|---------------------------|-----------------------------------------|\n| **Excitatory-first**  | $+a~(>0)$ (E)     | $b~(<0)$ (I)       | $x+a$ after E;<br> $x+a+b$ after I | Spikes if $x+a \\geq \\theta$ (resets before $b$ arrives), else not |\n| **Inhibitory-first**  | $b~(<0)$ (I)      | $+a~(>0)$ (E)      | $x+b$ after I;<br> $x+a+b$ after E | Spikes if $x+a+b \\ge"}}